.TH "OSTRYA\-REMOTE" "1" "" "ostrya" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ostrya\-remote \- Manage the configured remotes and their trusted GPG keys
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ostrya remote [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Manage the configured remotes and their trusted GPG keys.
.PP
Each remote is a \fB\%[remote "NAME"]\fR section in the \fB\%config\fR file of the repository. The trusted GPG keyring of a remote is the file \fB\%NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR at the root of the repository. A nested subcommand is necessary. Without one, the command fails before it opens the repository.
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
The global options are listed in \fBostrya\fR(1).
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "add"
Add a remote repository.
.PP
The command writes the \fB\%[remote "NAME"]\fR section of the \fB\%config\fR file of the repository, and prints nothing. It refuses a name that is not a valid remote name. If the remote exists, the command fails, unless \%\-\-if\-not\-exists or \%\-\-force is given.
.PP
.nf
ostrya remote add [OPTIONS] [NAME] [URL] [BRANCHES]...
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
\fINAME\fR
The remote name. Required, with the URL.
.IP
A name has one or more characters. Each character is a letter, a digit, \fB\%\-\fR, \fB\%_\fR, or \fB\%\&.\fR\&. The first character is a letter, a digit, or \fB\%_\fR\&.
.TP
\fIURL\fR
The base URL of the remote, or \fB\%metalink=URL\fR for a metalink remote.
.IP
The command writes a base URL to the \fB\%url\fR key. For \fB\%metalink=URL\fR, it writes URL to the \fB\%metalink\fR key and writes no \fB\%url\fR key. The pull of ostrya does not read the \fB\%metalink\fR key.
.IP
The command writes an \fB\%s3://BUCKET[/PREFIX]\fR address to the \fB\%pull\-url\fR and \fB\%push\-url\fR keys, after the branches, and writes no \fB\%url\fR key. It does not check the address. A pull or a push refuses a bad address when it uses the remote. \%\-\-set writes the \%S3 keys \fB\%s3\-region\fR, \fB\%s3\-endpoint\fR, \fB\%s3\-addressing\fR, and \fB\%s3\-profile\fR\&. \fB\%show\-url\fR and \fB\%list \-u\fR refuse a remote with no \fB\%url\fR key.
.TP
\fIBRANCHES...\fR
The refs that a pull of this remote takes if the pull names no ref.
.IP
The command writes them to the \fB\%branches\fR key.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-set\fR \fIKEY=VALUE\fR
Set this configuration key in the section of the remote.
.IP
The option can occur more than once. The command writes the pairs in the order of the command line. They come after the URL, the branches, \%\-\-contenturl, and \%\-\-custom\-backend, and before the keys of \%\-\-no\-gpg\-verify, \%\-\-no\-sign\-verify, \%\-\-sign\-verify, and \%\-\-collection\-id. A later write of a key replaces the earlier value. The command refuses a value with no \fB\%=\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-gpg\-verify\fR
Do not require a GPG signature on the commits pulled from this remote.
.IP
The command writes \fB\%gpg\-verify=false\fR\&. Without this option, a pull from the remote verifies the GPG signatures of its commits (see the key \fB\%gpg\-verify\fR in \%ostrya\-config(1)).
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-sign\-verify\fR
Do not require an \%ed25519, spki, or GPG signature on pulled commits.
.IP
The command writes \fB\%gpg\-verify=false\fR and \fB\%sign\-verify=false\fR\&. This option conflicts with \%\-\-sign\-verify.
.TP
\fB\-\-sign\-verify\fR \fIKEYTYPE=[inline|file]:PUBKEY\fR
Trust this public key for the \%ed25519 or the spki engine.
.IP
The value is \%KEYTYPE=inline:PUBKEY or \%KEYTYPE=file:PATH. KEYTYPE is \fB\%ed25519\fR, or \fB\%spki\fR in a build with the \fB\%spki\fR feature. The command writes the key \fB\%verification\-KEYTYPE\-key\fR for an inline key, or \fB\%verification\-KEYTYPE\-file\fR for a file. It then writes \fB\%sign\-verify\fR with the engine of each \%\-\-sign\-verify, separated by commas. The option can occur more than once, and it conflicts with \%\-\-no\-sign\-verify. An spki PUBKEY is the \%base64 of a SubjectPublicKeyInfo DER.
.TP
\fB\-\-if\-not\-exists\fR
If the remote exists, do nothing.
.IP
The command then also imports no keys for \%\-\-gpg\-import, and it exits \%0. This option conflicts with \%\-\-force.
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
If the remote exists, replace its section.
.IP
The command removes the full section and writes a new one. This option conflicts with \%\-\-if\-not\-exists.
.TP
\fB\-\-gpg\-import\fR \fIFILE\fR
Import the keys of this keyring file into the trusted keyring.
.IP
The import is the same as \fB\%remote gpg\-import\fR with \%\-\-keyring. The command imports the keys only after it writes the section of the remote, and then prints the number of imported keys.
.TP
\fB\-\-custom\-backend\fR \fINAME\fR
Name a program other than ostrya that fetches this remote.
.IP
The command writes NAME to the \fB\%custom\-backend\fR key. ostrya does not read this key.
.TP
\fB\-\-contenturl\fR \fIURL\fR
Write this URL to the \fB\%contenturl\fR key of the remote.
.IP
The key names a different URL for the content objects. The pull of ostrya does not read this key. It fetches all objects from the \fB\%pull\-url\fR or the \fB\%url\fR of the remote.
.TP
\fB\-\-collection\-id\fR \fICOLLECTION\-ID\fR
A globally unique id for the remote as a collection of refs.
.IP
The command writes the value to the \fB\%collection\-id\fR key of the remote.
.SS "init"
Create a bucket repository in an \%S3 bucket.
.PP
The command writes the \fB\%config\fR of a new bucket repository at the \fB\%s3://\fR address of NAME, or at the address \fB\%s3://BUCKET[/PREFIX]\fR\&. The \fB\%config\fR holds the group \fB\%[core]\fR with \fB\%repo_version=1\fR and \fB\%mode=archive\-z2\fR, and \fB\%collection\-id=ID\fR with \%\-\-collection\-id. A bucket repository with a collection id is the primary server of that collection, and a push of the refs of another collection writes them under \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/\fR\&. With \%\-\-zlib\-level, the group \fB\%[archive]\fR with \fB\%zlib\-level=LEVEL\fR follows. The command writes no ref and no summary, and prints nothing on success.
.PP
The command takes the writer lock of the bucket repository, writes the \fB\%config\fR only if the bucket repository holds none, and removes the lock. It refuses a bucket repository that holds a \fB\%config\fR, so the collection id of a bucket repository never changes. A push needs the \fB\%config\fR\&. The command needs the \%S3 permissions \fB\%s3:GetObject\fR, \fB\%s3:PutObject\fR, \fB\%s3:ListBucket\fR, and \fB\%s3:DeleteObject\fR\&.
.PP
With an address, the command needs no repository and reads no \fB\%\-\-repo\fR, current directory, or \fB\%OSTREE_REPO\fR\&. With a remote name, it reads the remote from the config of the repository. With \%\-\-break\-lock, an init that removed the writer lock of another writer writes one \fB\%warning: removed the writer lock of HOLDER\fR line to standard error.
.PP
.nf
ostrya remote init [OPTIONS] [NAME]
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
\fINAME\fR
The remote name or the address \fB\%s3://BUCKET[/PREFIX]\fR\&. Required.
.IP
The address of a remote is its \fB\%push\-url\fR key if that key holds an \fB\%s3://\fR address, else its \fB\%pull\-url\fR key if that key holds an \fB\%s3://\fR address. The command refuses a remote with neither, and never reads the \fB\%url\fR key.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-collection\-id\fR \fIID\fR
Write \fB\%collection\-id=ID\fR into the \fB\%config\fR of the bucket repository.
.IP
The command refuses an ID that fails the \%collection\-id rule or is longer than \%255 bytes, before any request.
.TP
\fB\-\-zlib\-level\fR \fILEVEL\fR
Write \fB\%[archive] zlib\-level=LEVEL\fR, \%1 to \%9, into the \fB\%config\fR of the bucket repository.
.IP
A push builds each \fB\%\&.filez\fR that it uploads at this level. Without this option, the \fB\%config\fR has no \fB\%[archive]\fR group, and a push takes the level \%6. The command refuses a value outside \%1 to \%9 before any request.
.TP
\fB\-\-break\-lock\fR
Remove the writer lock of another writer of the bucket repository.
.IP
The command reads the lock, deletes it, and then takes its own lock. It writes the holder of the removed lock to standard error after an init that succeeds. Use it only when the holder is gone, for example after a crash.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-region\fR \fIREGION\fR
The region of the signature of each request to the \%S3 server.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-region\fR key of the remote, over \fB\%AWS_REGION\fR and \fB\%AWS_DEFAULT_REGION\fR, and over the \fB\%region\fR of the AWS profile. The default is \fB\%us\-east\-1\fR\&. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR
The \fB\%http://\fR or \fB\%https://\fR URL of the \%S3 server, with no path.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-endpoint\fR key of the remote, over \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3\fR and \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL\fR, and over the endpoint of the AWS profile. Without each of them, the server is AWS. An \fB\%http://\fR URL needs \%\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-addressing\fR \fISTYLE\fR
The addressing style of the bucket: \fB\%path\fR or \fB\%virtual\fR\&.
.IP
\fB\%path\fR puts the bucket in the path of each request, and \fB\%virtual\fR puts it in the host name. The option wins over the \fB\%s3\-addressing\fR key of the remote and over the \fB\%addressing_style\fR of the AWS profile. The default is \fB\%virtual\fR for AWS and \fB\%path\fR for a set server. A bucket name that holds a \fB\%\&.\fR gets \fB\%path\fR by default. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&. Possible values: path, virtual.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-profile\fR \fIPROFILE\fR
The profile of the shared AWS files.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-profile\fR key of the remote and over \fB\%AWS_PROFILE\fR\&. A profile that this option or the key names skips the key pair, the region, and the endpoint of the environment. The default profile is \fB\%default\fR\&. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials\fR
Send the signature of each request to an \fB\%http://\fR \%S3 server.
.IP
The signature is a credential. Without this switch, the command refuses an \fB\%http://\fR \%S3 server before any request. Use it for a server on a loopback address or behind a proxy that terminates TLS. No key of the remote sets this switch. The command refuses it for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.SS "fsck"
Check a bucket repository in an \%S3 bucket.
.PP
The command reads the bucket repository at the \fB\%s3://\fR address of NAME, or at the address \fB\%s3://BUCKET[/PREFIX]\fR, and checks the \fB\%config\fR, the ref files, the summary, the keys under \fB\%objects/\fR, each commit, each object of the tree of each commit, and each composefs image of a commit. It reads each object that a commit reaches, so its cost is the download of these objects.
.PP
Unless \%\-q is given, the command first prints the counts: \fB\%Listed N objects\fR, \fB\%Checked C commits and O objects, B bytes\fR, and \fB\%N objects not reached by a commit\fR, then \fB\%N composefs images with no commit\fR and \fB\%N partial commits\fR when the count is not zero. It writes one line for each finding to standard error, sorted by key: \fB\%KIND KEY: DETAIL\fR, and \fB\%; in commits C1, C2\fR when the finding names commits. A note starts with \fB\%note: \fR\&. A report with an error ends with \fB\%error: Bucket repository corruption encountered\fR on standard error, and the command exits \%1. Else the command prints \fB\%fsck of the bucket repository ADDRESS: no errors found\fR, and exits \%0. A stale summary, an absent summary, a marker of a commit whose tree is whole, an incomplete multipart upload, and a writer lock are notes. A failed write to standard output gives an error line and exit \%1.
.PP
With a remote name, the command verifies the signature of the summary with the summary keys of the remote, as a pull with \fB\%gpg\-verify\-summary\fR or \fB\%sign\-verify\-summary\fR does. With an address, it verifies no signature.
.PP
A run without \%\-\-delete takes no lock, so a writer can change the bucket during the run. Before the command reports an object that the listing does not hold, it reads the object again: an object that is in the bucket now gives a note, and an object whose commits are all gone now gives no line.
.PP
The command checks the dependencies in field \%2 of each commit. A dependency that is absent, partial, or defective gives a \fB\%dependency\fR error on the key of the commit that names it. With \%\-\-delete, the command checks the dependencies against the bucket after the repair, and writes and deletes no key for such an error.
.PP
With \%\-\-delete, the command takes the writer lock, writes the marker \fB\%state/<checksum>.commitpartial\fR for each commit that reaches a bad or a missing object of a tree, deletes the marker of each commit whose tree is whole, and deletes each bad object. It prints \fB\%Marking commit as partial: COMMIT\fR with the refs of the commit, \fB\%Deleted the partial\-commit marker of commit COMMIT\fR, \fB\%Deleted KEY\fR for each deleted object, and \fB\%Deleted the commit object of a ref: COMMIT\fR with the refs of each deleted commit that a ref names. A push of a ref whose commit has a marker uploads the missing objects of its tree, and removes the marker. The command refuses \%\-\-delete for a bucket repository with no valid \fB\%config\fR, and then changes nothing.
.PP
If the repair fails after its first change, the command prints the report with the changes that completed, then an error line that states that the repair is incomplete, and exits \%1. Run the command with \%\-\-delete again before a push: until then, a marker can be in the bucket while the bad objects of its commit stay, and a push of the commit then uploads no object for them and removes the marker.
.PP
The command needs the \%S3 permissions \fB\%s3:GetObject\fR, \fB\%s3:ListBucket\fR, and \fB\%s3:ListBucketMultipartUploads\fR\&. With \%\-\-delete, it also needs \fB\%s3:PutObject\fR and \fB\%s3:DeleteObject\fR\&.
.PP
With an address, the command needs no repository and reads no \fB\%\-\-repo\fR, current directory, or \fB\%OSTREE_REPO\fR\&. With a remote name, it reads the remote from the config of the repository.
.PP
.nf
ostrya remote fsck [OPTIONS] [NAME]
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
\fINAME\fR
The remote name or the address \fB\%s3://BUCKET[/PREFIX]\fR\&. Required.
.IP
The address of a remote is its \fB\%push\-url\fR key if that key holds an \fB\%s3://\fR address, else its \fB\%pull\-url\fR key if that key holds an \fB\%s3://\fR address. The command refuses a remote with neither, and never reads the \fB\%url\fR key.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-delete\fR
Repair the bucket repository.
.IP
The command takes the writer lock, writes a \%partial\-commit marker for each commit that reaches a bad or a missing object of a tree, deletes the marker of each commit whose tree is whole, and then deletes each object whose bytes do not match its name or do not parse. It deletes no commit because of a bad object of its tree.
.TP
\fB\-\-break\-lock\fR
Remove the writer lock of another writer of the bucket repository.
.IP
The command reads the lock, deletes it, and then takes its own lock. It writes the holder of the removed lock to standard error after the report of the run. Use it only when the holder is gone, for example after a crash.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Do not print the counts.
.IP
The command still prints the findings, the actions of \%\-\-delete, and the final result line.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-region\fR \fIREGION\fR
The region of the signature of each request to the \%S3 server.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-region\fR key of the remote, over \fB\%AWS_REGION\fR and \fB\%AWS_DEFAULT_REGION\fR, and over the \fB\%region\fR of the AWS profile. The default is \fB\%us\-east\-1\fR\&. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR
The \fB\%http://\fR or \fB\%https://\fR URL of the \%S3 server, with no path.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-endpoint\fR key of the remote, over \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3\fR and \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL\fR, and over the endpoint of the AWS profile. Without each of them, the server is AWS. An \fB\%http://\fR URL needs \%\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-addressing\fR \fISTYLE\fR
The addressing style of the bucket: \fB\%path\fR or \fB\%virtual\fR\&.
.IP
\fB\%path\fR puts the bucket in the path of each request, and \fB\%virtual\fR puts it in the host name. The option wins over the \fB\%s3\-addressing\fR key of the remote and over the \fB\%addressing_style\fR of the AWS profile. The default is \fB\%virtual\fR for AWS and \fB\%path\fR for a set server. A bucket name that holds a \fB\%\&.\fR gets \fB\%path\fR by default. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&. Possible values: path, virtual.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-profile\fR \fIPROFILE\fR
The profile of the shared AWS files.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-profile\fR key of the remote and over \fB\%AWS_PROFILE\fR\&. A profile that this option or the key names skips the key pair, the region, and the endpoint of the environment. The default profile is \fB\%default\fR\&. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials\fR
Send the signature of each request to an \fB\%http://\fR \%S3 server.
.IP
The signature is a credential. Without this switch, the command refuses an \fB\%http://\fR \%S3 server before any request. Use it for a server on a loopback address or behind a proxy that terminates TLS. No key of the remote sets this switch. The command refuses it for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.SS "prune"
Delete the unreachable objects of a bucket repository in an \%S3 bucket.
.PP
The command prunes the bucket repository at the \fB\%s3://\fR address of NAME, or at the address \fB\%s3://BUCKET[/PREFIX]\fR, as \fB\%ostrya prune\fR prunes a local repository with the same options. The commit of each ref is a root. Without \%\-\-refs\-only and \%\-\-keep\-younger\-than, each commit of the bucket is a root too. The commits that the summary names are roots, and the command keeps them with no parent. A kept commit keeps its detached metadata and its composefs image.
.PP
The dependencies in field \%2 of each commit that the walk reaches are also roots, as in \fB\%ostrya prune\fR\&. With \%\-\-refs\-only or \%\-\-keep\-younger\-than, a dependency gets the bound of \%\-\-depth or \%\-\-keep\-younger\-than, also a dependency of a commit that the summary names. Without them, each commit of the bucket is a root that keeps no parent, and a dependency keeps no parent too. The command reads field \%2 from the commit object, with no other request. An entry that names a commit that is not in the bucket gives no error, and the command keeps the detached metadata and the composefs image of that commit if they are in the bucket. For each defective field \%2 entry, the command writes one \fB\%warning: related commits in field 2 of commit CHECKSUM: REASON\fR line to standard error before the lines on standard output, and the defective entry roots nothing.
.PP
The command reads no detached metadata, so it reads no build source in the detached metadata key \fB\%ostrya.related\fR\&. It deletes a build source that no root reaches, which \fB\%ostrya prune\fR keeps. Without \%\-\-refs\-only and \%\-\-keep\-younger\-than, the command also deletes the detached metadata and the composefs image of a parent commit that the bucket does not hold, which \fB\%ostrya prune\fR can keep.
.PP
The command deletes each object that no root reaches, then each static delta and each \fB\%state/<commit>.commitpartial\fR marker whose commit is not in the bucket after the run, and aborts each incomplete multipart upload. It deletes the commit objects first, so a run that stops leaves a bucket repository that a reader can use, and the next run deletes the rest. It deletes no ref, no \fB\%\&.tombstone\-commit\fR, and no key under \fB\%delta\-indexes/\fR\&.
.PP
The command reads each commit and dirtree that the walk reaches with one GET, and checks its checksum. It reads the commits one at a time, and at most \%8 dirtrees of the tree of one commit at a time. A commit or a dirtree whose bytes do not match its name stops the command before its first delete. Run \fB\%ostrya remote fsck \-\-delete\fR to repair such an object.
.PP
The command writes no summary, and keeps each commit that the summary names. A summary that names a deleted static delta stays as it is until \fB\%ostrya remote summary \-u\fR writes the summary again.
.PP
The command prints the lines of \fB\%ostrya prune\fR: \fB\%Total objects: N\fR, then \fB\%Deleted N objects, SIZE freed\fR, or with \%\-\-no\-prune \fB\%Would delete: N objects, freeing SIZE\fR, or \fB\%No unreachable objects\fR\&. The counts leave out the detached metadata, the composefs images, and the static deltas. Then, for each count that is not zero, it prints \fB\%Deleted N static deltas\fR, \fB\%Deleted N partial\-commit markers\fR, and \fB\%Aborted N incomplete multipart uploads\fR, or with \%\-\-no\-prune \fB\%Would delete N static deltas\fR, \fB\%Would delete N partial\-commit markers\fR, and \fB\%Would abort N incomplete multipart uploads\fR\&.
.PP
The command takes the writer lock of the bucket repository, also with \%\-\-no\-prune, and removes it at the end. It needs the \%S3 permissions of a push, \fB\%s3:GetObject\fR, \fB\%s3:PutObject\fR, \fB\%s3:ListBucket\fR, \fB\%s3:DeleteObject\fR, and \fB\%s3:AbortMultipartUpload\fR, and also \fB\%s3:ListBucketMultipartUploads\fR\&. It refuses a bucket repository whose \fB\%config\fR sets \fB\%[core] tombstone\-commits\fR to true.
.PP
With an address, the command needs no repository and reads no \fB\%\-\-repo\fR, current directory, or \fB\%OSTREE_REPO\fR\&. With a remote name, it reads the remote from the config of the repository. With \%\-\-break\-lock, a prune that removed the writer lock of another writer writes one \fB\%warning: removed the writer lock of HOLDER\fR line to standard error, after the other lines.
.PP
.nf
ostrya remote prune [OPTIONS] [NAME]
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
\fINAME\fR
The remote name or the address \fB\%s3://BUCKET[/PREFIX]\fR\&. Required.
.IP
The address of a remote is its \fB\%push\-url\fR key if that key holds an \fB\%s3://\fR address, else its \fB\%pull\-url\fR key if that key holds an \fB\%s3://\fR address. The command refuses a remote with neither, and never reads the \fB\%url\fR key.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-refs\-only\fR
Delete each commit and object that no ref reaches.
.IP
Without this option, each commit of the bucket is a root of the walk. A commit that no ref names then stays, with the objects that it reaches.
.TP
\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIDEPTH\fR
The number of parents of each ref to keep.
.IP
The value \%\-1 keeps all history. The value \%0 keeps only the commit that the ref names, and so does each negative value other than \%\-1. The bound deletes commits only if the walk starts from the refs alone: with \%\-\-refs\-only or \%\-\-keep\-younger\-than. Default: \%\-1.
.TP
\fB\-\-keep\-younger\-than\fR \fIDATE\fR
Delete the history of each ref that is older than DATE.
.IP
DATE is \fB\%@SECONDS\fR since the Unix epoch, or a date and time with a UTC offset, for example \fB\%2024\-05\-01T12:00:00Z\fR or \fB\%2024\-05\-01 12:00+02:00\fR\&. The command refuses each other form before any request. The commit that a ref names stays at all ages. The walk starts from the refs alone, as with \%\-\-refs\-only. This bound replaces \%\-\-depth.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-prune\fR
Compute and print the statistics, and delete nothing.
.IP
The command still takes and removes the writer lock, so it needs the permissions of a writer. This option conflicts with \%\-\-break\-lock.
.TP
\fB\-\-break\-lock\fR
Remove the writer lock of another writer of the bucket repository.
.IP
The command reads the lock, deletes it, and then takes its own lock. It writes the holder of the removed lock to standard error after a prune that succeeds. Use it only when the holder is gone, for example after a crash. This option conflicts with \%\-\-no\-prune.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-region\fR \fIREGION\fR
The region of the signature of each request to the \%S3 server.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-region\fR key of the remote, over \fB\%AWS_REGION\fR and \fB\%AWS_DEFAULT_REGION\fR, and over the \fB\%region\fR of the AWS profile. The default is \fB\%us\-east\-1\fR\&. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR
The \fB\%http://\fR or \fB\%https://\fR URL of the \%S3 server, with no path.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-endpoint\fR key of the remote, over \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3\fR and \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL\fR, and over the endpoint of the AWS profile. Without each of them, the server is AWS. An \fB\%http://\fR URL needs \%\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-addressing\fR \fISTYLE\fR
The addressing style of the bucket: \fB\%path\fR or \fB\%virtual\fR\&.
.IP
\fB\%path\fR puts the bucket in the path of each request, and \fB\%virtual\fR puts it in the host name. The option wins over the \fB\%s3\-addressing\fR key of the remote and over the \fB\%addressing_style\fR of the AWS profile. The default is \fB\%virtual\fR for AWS and \fB\%path\fR for a set server. A bucket name that holds a \fB\%\&.\fR gets \fB\%path\fR by default. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&. Possible values: path, virtual.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-profile\fR \fIPROFILE\fR
The profile of the shared AWS files.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-profile\fR key of the remote and over \fB\%AWS_PROFILE\fR\&. A profile that this option or the key names skips the key pair, the region, and the endpoint of the environment. The default profile is \fB\%default\fR\&. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials\fR
Send the signature of each request to an \fB\%http://\fR \%S3 server.
.IP
The signature is a credential. Without this switch, the command refuses an \fB\%http://\fR \%S3 server before any request. Use it for a server on a loopback address or behind a proxy that terminates TLS. No key of the remote sets this switch. The command refuses it for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.SS "delete"
Delete a remote repository and its trusted GPG keyring.
.PP
The command removes the keyring \fB\%NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR at the root of the repository, and then the \fB\%[remote "NAME"]\fR section of \fB\%config\fR\&. It prints nothing. If the remote does not exist, the command fails, unless \%\-\-if\-exists is given.
.PP
.nf
ostrya remote delete [OPTIONS] [NAME]
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
\fINAME\fR
The remote to delete. Required.
.IP
The command refuses a name that \fB\%remote add\fR refuses.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-if\-exists\fR
If the remote does not exist, do nothing.
.SS "list"
List the configured remote names, sorted.
.PP
The command prints one name on each line. With \%\-u, the URL of each remote follows its name.
.PP
.nf
ostrya remote list [OPTIONS]
.fi
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-show\-urls\fR
Print the URL of each remote after its name.
.IP
The command pads each name with spaces to the length of the longest name plus two, so the URLs align. A remote with no \fB\%url\fR key stops the listing with an error. The names before it stay printed.
.SS "show\-url"
Print the URL of a remote.
.PP
The command prints the value of the \fB\%url\fR key of the remote. It fails if the remote does not exist, or if the remote has no \fB\%url\fR key. A remote that a metalink describes has no \fB\%url\fR key.
.PP
.nf
ostrya remote show\-url [OPTIONS] [NAME]
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
\fINAME\fR
The remote to report. Required.
.SS "refs"
List the refs that a remote publishes in its summary.
.PP
The command fetches the summary of the remote over HTTP or ssh, as a pull does. From an \fB\%s3://\fR address in \fB\%pull\-url\fR, it reads the summary of the bucket repository with signed requests. It prints one \fB\%NAME:REF\fR line for each ref, in the order of the summary. It does not verify the signature of the summary. If the remote has no summary file, the command fails. A push to a bucket repository writes no summary, and \fB\%ostrya remote summary \-u\fR writes it.
.PP
.nf
ostrya remote refs [OPTIONS] [NAME]
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
\fINAME\fR
The remote to list. Required.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-revision\fR
Print the commit checksum of each ref after a tab.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-region\fR \fIREGION\fR
The region of the signature of each request to the \%S3 server.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-region\fR key of the remote, over \fB\%AWS_REGION\fR and \fB\%AWS_DEFAULT_REGION\fR, and over the \fB\%region\fR of the AWS profile. The default is \fB\%us\-east\-1\fR\&. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR
The \fB\%http://\fR or \fB\%https://\fR URL of the \%S3 server, with no path.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-endpoint\fR key of the remote, over \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3\fR and \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL\fR, and over the endpoint of the AWS profile. Without each of them, the server is AWS. An \fB\%http://\fR URL needs \%\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-addressing\fR \fISTYLE\fR
The addressing style of the bucket: \fB\%path\fR or \fB\%virtual\fR\&.
.IP
\fB\%path\fR puts the bucket in the path of each request, and \fB\%virtual\fR puts it in the host name. The option wins over the \fB\%s3\-addressing\fR key of the remote and over the \fB\%addressing_style\fR of the AWS profile. The default is \fB\%virtual\fR for AWS and \fB\%path\fR for a set server. A bucket name that holds a \fB\%\&.\fR gets \fB\%path\fR by default. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&. Possible values: path, virtual.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-profile\fR \fIPROFILE\fR
The profile of the shared AWS files.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-profile\fR key of the remote and over \fB\%AWS_PROFILE\fR\&. A profile that this option or the key names skips the key pair, the region, and the endpoint of the environment. The default profile is \fB\%default\fR\&. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials\fR
Send the signature of each request to an \fB\%http://\fR \%S3 server.
.IP
The signature is a credential. Without this switch, the command refuses an \fB\%http://\fR \%S3 server before any request. Use it for a server on a loopback address or behind a proxy that terminates TLS. No key of the remote sets this switch. The command refuses it for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.SS "summary"
Report the summary of a remote, or write the summary of a bucket repository.
.PP
The command fetches the summary of the remote over HTTP or ssh, as a pull does. From an \fB\%s3://\fR address in \fB\%pull\-url\fR, it reads the summary of the bucket repository with signed requests. It does not verify the signature of the summary. If the remote has no summary file, the command fails. A push to a bucket repository writes no summary, and \%\-u writes it.
.PP
The default report gives each ref with its commit size, its commit checksum, and its metadata. The global metadata of the summary follows the refs. The report shows each timestamp in UTC.
.PP
With \%\-u, the command writes the summary of the bucket repository at the \fB\%s3://\fR address of NAME, or at the address \fB\%s3://BUCKET[/PREFIX]\fR, and prints nothing. This is an ostrya extension, and a build without the feature \fB\%s3\fR does not have it. The summary lists each ref of \fB\%refs/heads/\fR, and the refs of each \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/\fR in its collection map. It names each static delta with a superblock. Its bytes are the bytes of the root summary that \fB\%ostrya summary \-u\fR writes for a local repository with the same refs, commits, deltas, and config, when that command writes no \fB\%ostree\-metadata\fR anchor commit. The command reads the commit of each ref from the bucket, and checks its checksum. It refuses a ref whose commit the bucket does not hold, a key under \fB\%refs/\fR outside \fB\%refs/heads/\fR and \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/\fR, and a ref name that is a directory of another ref name, as \fB\%a\fR is of \fB\%a/b\fR\&.
.PP
Each \%\-\-gpg\-sign key, each \%\-\-sign key, and each \%\-\-keys\-file key signs the summary, in this order. The command writes \fB\%summary\fR, and then \fB\%summary.sig\fR when it has a key. Between the two writes, a pull that verifies the summary refuses it. If the write of \fB\%summary.sig\fR fails after the write of \fB\%summary\fR, the command fails with an error that states that the two keys can fail to match, and a run again with the same keys writes a matching pair. The command never deletes \fB\%summary.sig\fR, so a bucket repository that holds one refuses an update with no signing key.
.PP
The command takes the writer lock of the bucket repository, and removes it at the end. It needs the \%S3 permissions \fB\%s3:GetObject\fR, \fB\%s3:PutObject\fR, \fB\%s3:ListBucket\fR, and \fB\%s3:DeleteObject\fR\&. With an address, it needs no repository and reads no \fB\%\-\-repo\fR, current directory, or \fB\%OSTREE_REPO\fR\&. With a remote name, it reads the remote from the config of the repository. With \%\-\-break\-lock, an update that removed the writer lock of another writer writes one \fB\%warning: removed the writer lock of HOLDER\fR line to standard error.
.PP
.nf
ostrya remote summary [OPTIONS] [NAME]
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
\fINAME\fR
The remote to report. Required.
.IP
With \%\-u, the remote name or the address \fB\%s3://BUCKET[/PREFIX]\fR\&. The address of a remote is then its \fB\%push\-url\fR key if that key holds an \fB\%s3://\fR address, else its \fB\%pull\-url\fR key if that key holds an \fB\%s3://\fR address. The command refuses a remote with neither, and never reads the \fB\%url\fR key.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-list\-metadata\-keys\fR
List the available metadata keys.
.IP
The command prints the keys of the global metadata of the summary, sorted, one key on each line. \%\-\-raw takes precedence over this option.
.TP
\fB\-\-print\-metadata\-key\fR \fIKEY\fR
Print the value of one metadata key.
.IP
The command prints the value in the GVariant text form, with its type. If the global metadata has no such key, the command fails. \%\-\-raw and \%\-\-list\-metadata\-keys take precedence over this option.
.TP
\fB\-\-raw\fR
Show the raw variant data.
.IP
The command prints the full summary in the GVariant text form, with the \%big\-endian fields converted.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-update\fR
Write the summary of the bucket repository of NAME, or at the address NAME.
.IP
This is an ostrya extension. The option conflicts with \%\-\-raw, \%\-\-list\-metadata\-keys, and \%\-\-print\-metadata\-key. The command prints nothing.
.TP
\fB\-\-gpg\-sign\fR \fIKEY\-ID\fR
With \%\-u, sign the summary with the GPG key that this selector names.
.IP
The option uses the gpg engine, and it is not a key of a \%\-\-sign\-type. Each occurrence adds one signature. The option needs \%\-u.
.TP
\fB\-\-gpg\-homedir\fR \fIDIR\fR
With \%\-u, the GnuPG home directory where gpg resolves signing keys.
.IP
Only the gpg engine reads it: for \%\-\-gpg\-sign, and for \%\-\-sign under the gpg engine. The option needs \%\-u.
.TP
\fB\-\-sign\fR \fIKEY\-ID\fR
With \%\-u, sign the summary with this key.
.IP
The \%\-\-sign\-type before the key selects its engine. For \%ed25519 and spki, the key is the \%base64 of the secret key. An spki secret key is a \%PKCS#8 DER, a \%SEC1 DER that names the \%P\-256 or the \%P\-384 curve, or a \%PKCS#1 DER of an RSA key, and its \%base64 can hold line breaks. For gpg, the key is a selector, as for \%\-\-gpg\-sign. Each occurrence adds one signature. The option needs \%\-u.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-sign\-type\fR \fINAME\fR
With \%\-u, the signature engine of the \%\-\-sign and \%\-\-keys\-file keys after it.
.IP
The names are \fB\%ed25519\fR, \fB\%spki\fR, and \fB\%gpg\fR, and the command refuses any other name. Each key takes the engine of the nearest \%\-\-sign\-type before it on the command line. A key before the first \%\-\-sign\-type takes \fB\%ed25519\fR\&. The option can occur more than once, and each occurrence needs a key of its own: a key after it and before the next \%\-\-sign\-type. \%\-\-gpg\-sign is not a key of this option. The option needs \%\-u.
.TP
\fB\-\-keys\-file\fR \fIPATH\fR
With \%\-u, sign the summary with the keys of this file, in the format of \fB\%ostrya sign\fR\&.
.IP
For \%ed25519, the file has \%base64 secret keys, one per line. For spki, the file holds PEM \fB\%PRIVATE KEY\fR blocks, each a \%PKCS#8 DER of a \%P\-256, a \%P\-384, an \%Ed25519, or an RSA key. The \%\-\-sign\-type before the file selects its engine. The command reads every file, and each key adds one signature after each \%\-\-sign key. The gpg engine refuses this option. The option needs \%\-u.
.TP
\fB\-\-break\-lock\fR
With \%\-u, remove the writer lock of another writer of the bucket repository.
.IP
The command reads the lock, deletes it, and then takes its own lock. It writes the holder of the removed lock to standard error after an update that succeeds. Use it only when the holder is gone, for example after a crash. The option needs \%\-u.
.TP
\fB\-\-last\-modified\fR \fISECONDS\fR
With \%\-u, set \fB\%ostree.summary.last\-modified\fR, in seconds since the Unix epoch.
.IP
A fixed value makes the output reproducible. Without this option, the command uses the current time. \fB\%SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH\fR does not change this value. The option needs \%\-u.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-region\fR \fIREGION\fR
The region of the signature of each request to the \%S3 server.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-region\fR key of the remote, over \fB\%AWS_REGION\fR and \fB\%AWS_DEFAULT_REGION\fR, and over the \fB\%region\fR of the AWS profile. The default is \fB\%us\-east\-1\fR\&. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR
The \fB\%http://\fR or \fB\%https://\fR URL of the \%S3 server, with no path.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-endpoint\fR key of the remote, over \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3\fR and \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL\fR, and over the endpoint of the AWS profile. Without each of them, the server is AWS. An \fB\%http://\fR URL needs \%\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-addressing\fR \fISTYLE\fR
The addressing style of the bucket: \fB\%path\fR or \fB\%virtual\fR\&.
.IP
\fB\%path\fR puts the bucket in the path of each request, and \fB\%virtual\fR puts it in the host name. The option wins over the \fB\%s3\-addressing\fR key of the remote and over the \fB\%addressing_style\fR of the AWS profile. The default is \fB\%virtual\fR for AWS and \fB\%path\fR for a set server. A bucket name that holds a \fB\%\&.\fR gets \fB\%path\fR by default. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&. Possible values: path, virtual.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-profile\fR \fIPROFILE\fR
The profile of the shared AWS files.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-profile\fR key of the remote and over \fB\%AWS_PROFILE\fR\&. A profile that this option or the key names skips the key pair, the region, and the endpoint of the environment. The default profile is \fB\%default\fR\&. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials\fR
Send the signature of each request to an \fB\%http://\fR \%S3 server.
.IP
The signature is a credential. Without this switch, the command refuses an \fB\%http://\fR \%S3 server before any request. Use it for a server on a loopback address or behind a proxy that terminates TLS. No key of the remote sets this switch. The command refuses it for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.SS "gpg\-import"
Import GPG keys into the trusted keyring of a remote.
.PP
The keyring is the file \fB\%NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR at the root of the repository. The keys come from the \%\-\-keyring files or from standard input. A key source is a binary or an \%ASCII\-armored public keyring. The command prints the number of keys that the keyring did not hold before.
.PP
A key that the keyring holds stays as it is. The command replaces it only for a key revocation that it can verify, or for a later expiry time of the key.
.PP
.nf
ostrya remote gpg\-import [OPTIONS] [NAME] [KEY_IDS]...
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
\fINAME\fR
The remote to import into. Required.
.TP
\fIKEY_IDS...\fR
The keys to import, by fingerprint, key id, or user id.
.IP
With no \%KEY_IDS, the command imports each key of the source. The command reads a key name as \fB\%gpg \-\-export\fR reads it. A run of \%8 or \%16 hex digits is a key id, and a run of \%32, \%40, or \%64 hex digits is a fingerprint. Each other value matches a part of a user id, and the match ignores the case of ASCII letters. If a value names no key of the source, the command fails.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-keyring\fR \fIFILE\fR
Import the keys of this keyring file.
.IP
The file is a binary or an \%ASCII\-armored public keyring. The option can occur more than once, and it conflicts with \%\-\-stdin.
.TP
\fB\-\-stdin\fR
Import the keys from standard input.
.IP
This option conflicts with \%\-\-keyring.
.SS "gpg\-list\-keys"
List the GPG keys in the trusted keyring of a remote.
.PP
The command reads the file \fB\%NAME.trustedkeys.gpg\fR at the root of the repository. For each key, it prints a \fB\%Key:\fR line with the fingerprint, a \fB\%Created:\fR line with the creation time in UTC, and one \fB\%UID:\fR line for each user id. If the keyring does not exist, the command prints nothing.
.PP
.nf
ostrya remote gpg\-list\-keys [OPTIONS] [NAME]
.fi
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
\fINAME\fR
The remote to report. Required.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBostrya\fR(1)
